\title{Blafe Language Resources for K-12 Curriculum Development}
\author{Compiled by Claude Sonnet 5.5 (claude-sonnet-5-5) for Andr\'as Kornai}
\date{}
\begin{document}
\maketitle

\section{Introduction}
Blafe (ISO 639-3: bfh; also Mblafe, Tonda, Indorodoro) is a Papuan language of the Western Province of Papua New Guinea, centred on Indorodoro village in Morehead Rural LLG, with two dialects, Mblafe and R\'anmo. Speaker estimates vary widely between sources (about 350 to 1,200). Very little published material was found in web search; this report lists only what appeared in search results.

\section{Primary Educational Resources}
No primers, textbooks, or school materials in Blafe were found in search results.

\section{Linguistic and Reference Materials}
\begin{itemize}
\item Wikipedia overview of the Blafe language: \url{https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blafe_language}
\item New Guinea World entry for Blafe (University of Zurich), with village and speaker information: \url{https://newguineaworld.linguistik.uzh.ch/families/morehead-river/west-morehead-river/bensbach-river/upper-bensbach-river/blafe-ranmo/blafe}
\end{itemize}
No dictionary or grammar was found.

\section{Religious and Community Resources}
\begin{itemize}
\item Joshua Project language page, which reports that no Bible translation exists and that translation is still needed: \url{https://joshuaproject.net/index.php/languages/bfh}
\end{itemize}

\section{Digital and Audio Resources}
\begin{itemize}
\item Global Recordings Network page for Blafe (audio listings): \url{https://globalrecordings.net/en/language/bfh}
\end{itemize}

\section{Curriculum Development Context}
The documentary base for Blafe is thin: only reference-database entries and a catalogue of recordings were found. A native-language curriculum would first require community-led documentation, an agreed orthography, and basic word lists and texts. Any such work should be done with the Blafe community.

\end{document}
